namespace trion:
    use formal

    section "Composite vocabulary abbreviates simple sentences":
        An expansion means the set of simple sentences that a composite sentence abbreviates.
        Each composite sentence has exactly one expansion.
        The reading of a composite sentence is the contracts of the simple sentences of the expansion of the composite sentence.

        [COMPOSITE-ABBREVIATES]
        The trion processor shall read each composite sentence as the contracts of the simple sentences of the expansion of the composite sentence.

        [COMPOSITE-NO-MEANING]
        The trion processor shall add no contract to the contracts of the expansion of a composite sentence.

        example COMPOSITE-SENTENCE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "The API shall store the message and log the message."
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|store message) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|store message) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|store message))"
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|log message) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|log message) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|log message))"

    section "A shared condition":
        A document structure means a part of a source file that writes statements.
        A shared condition means a document structure that states exactly one condition for a group of statements.
        A shared condition writes each statement of the group with the condition.

        example SHARED-CONDITION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
When the bell rings:
    [A] the API shall stop.
    [B] the API shall log the call.
"""
            expect "assume: Event(bell ring)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop))"
            expect "assume: Event(bell ring)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|log call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|log call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|log call))"

    section "A shared assumption is merging":
        Conjunction means the combination of contracts that requires each guarantee where the assumption of that guarantee holds.
        Merging means the combination of contracts that requires each guarantee where each assumption holds.
        A shared assumption means the assumption of a shared condition or the assumption of an assumption sentence.
        A distributed contract means a contract that holds a shared assumption in the assumption of the contract.
        The merging of a shared assumption is the conjunction of the distributed contracts of the shared assumption.
        The scope of an assumption sentence means the statements that the assumption sentence constrains.
        A shared condition narrows the scope of each assumption sentence of the shared condition to the statements of the shared condition.

        [COMPOSITE-CONJUNCTION]
        The trion processor shall read the statements of a specification as the conjunction of the contracts of the statements.

        [COMPOSITE-MERGING]
        The trion processor shall read a shared condition as the merging of the condition with each statement of the shared condition.

        [COMPOSITE-ASSUMPTION-DISTRIBUTED]
        The trion processor shall add the assumption of each assumption sentence to the assumption of each guarantee sentence in the scope of the assumption sentence.

        [COMPOSITE-ASSUMPTION-NOT-ALONE]
        The trion processor shall not read an assumption sentence as a separate contract.

    section "A selection and a sequence stay one contract":
        A selection means a simple sentence that offers alternatives.
        A sequence means a simple sentence that orders actions.
        An action list means a simple sentence that lists actions under exactly one modal verb.
        The operator of an action list is conjunction, inclusive choice, exclusive choice, or order.

        [SELECTION-ONE-CONTRACT]
        The trion processor shall read each selection and each sequence as exactly one contract.

        The guarantee of a selection is a disjunction.

        example ONE-OR-MORE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
The API shall perform one or more of the following actions:
    accept the call;
    reject the call;
"""
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: ((Act(API|accept call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|accept call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|accept call)) ∨ (Act(API|reject call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|reject call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|reject call)))"

        An action list of the exclusive choice operator excludes each further alternative.

        example EXACTLY-ONE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
The API shall perform exactly one of the following actions:
    accept the call;
    reject the call;
"""
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (((Act(API|accept call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|accept call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|accept call)) ∧ ¬(Act(API|reject call))) ∨ ((Act(API|reject call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|reject call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|reject call)) ∧ ¬(Act(API|accept call))))"

        The guarantee of a sequence orders the completions of the actions.

        example IN-ORDER:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
The API shall perform the following actions in order:
    stop;
    log the stop;
"""
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: ((Act(API|stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop)) ∧ (Act(API|log stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|log stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|log stop)) ∧ Before(API|stop, API|log stop))"

        An action list of the conjunction operator requires each action of the action list.

        example ALL:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
The API shall perform all of the following actions:
    stop;
    log the stop;
"""
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: ((Act(API|stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop)) ∧ (Act(API|log stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|log stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|log stop)))"

    section "An exception narrows the assumption":
        An exception means a clause that begins with the word "unless".
        The assumption of a simple sentence that has an exception holds the negation of that exception.

        example UNLESS:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "When the door closes, the door shall become locked, unless the key is present."
            expect "assume: (Event(door close) ∧ ¬Val(key, present))"
            expect "guarantee: Val(door, locked)"
